course: dictionary
lesson: 7387
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives each meaning a number, and the words around the word tell you which meaning the writer chose. Ask yourself which meaning fits best in the sentence.
explain: Take «a plume of smoke». Smoke cannot wear decoration, so here plume means a long cloud that rises and spreads out. A knight's helmet with a plume on top is different: the plume is a bunch of soft feathers worn to look grand. Same word, two different things.
explain: Plume can also be a verb, but it is rare. A bird that plumes is tidying its feathers. You will meet the noun far more often, so start there.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does «a plume of ash» still work if you put in «a feather of ash»? It does not, so the cloud meaning is the right one.
fact: A plume is a large, soft, beautiful feather, or a bunch of feathers worn as decoration.
fact: A plume is also a long cloud of smoke, steam, dust or water that rises and spreads out.
fact: «A plume of ash» can rise from a volcano, and «a plume of steam» can rise from hot water.
fact: In stories, knights and soldiers often wear plumes on their helmets, and peacocks show off their plumes.
fact: In French, «prête-moi ta plume» means "lend me your pen", where plume means an old feather pen.
exercise: «The volcano sent up a plume of ash.» What is a plume here? | A bird's nest | A dry, dusty road | *A long cloud that rises and spreads out | A soft feather worn on a hat
exercise: «The knight's helmet had a red plume on top.» What is the plume? | A long cloud of smoke | A sharp piece of metal | *A bunch of feathers worn as decoration | A puddle of water
exercise: Ellie says plume only ever means smoke. Is she right? | No, it means a shiny rock | Yes, but only in France | Yes, plume only ever means smoke | *No, a plume can also be a feather worn as decoration
test: «The whale breathed out and made a plume of spray.» What does plume mean here? | A new kind of boat | *A rising, spreading cloud of water spray | A deep hole in the sea | A sleepy whale
test: Which phrase uses plume to mean a feather decoration? | *«a feathered plume» | «a plume of spray» | «a plume of smoke» | «a plume of steam»
exam: A reader sees «a plume of hot rock rising from deep in the mantle». Which meaning fits best? | A sleepy bird | A small pebble | *A long, rising column of something | A soft feather
